{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":70855,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"52bf8a49-9c64-5767-9bfe-a2e32b5366d6","total_cost_usd":0.543004,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9930,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6795,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9930,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6795,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9930,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9930},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4539,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004633999999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6795,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9930,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.53837,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-k1-reviewer-fable-48\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:0de3a6061d3bb4056306\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"The selected result reports a two-sample Student t-test with t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44 computed on all 40 chamber-visit rows (n = 20 per management class), even though the declared independent unit is plot_id and there are only 10 plots (5 per class), each contributing 4 repeated readings from the same patch of peat. Each independent unit therefore enters the row-independent t-test four times, inflating the effective sample size and degrees of freedom (38 instead of 8 at plot level), so the reported test statistic, p-value, and effect size are not supported at the declared unit of independence. This finding is bounded to the inferential quantities: the direction of the difference (restored > drained) is consistent with plot-level means and is not itself contested.\",\"root_cause\":\"workflow/analysis.py never uses the plot_id column: flux_by_group() appends every CSV row's ch4_flux_mg_m2_h value to a per-management list, and stats.ttest_ind() is applied directly to these row-level lists (20 values per group). No aggregation to one value per plot (or any repeated-measures/mixed-model structure) is performed, so each of the 10 independent plots is entered four times into a procedure whose validity assumes each entry is an independent observation.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description declares the independent unit to be plot_id and states that one row is a single visit-level reading, with each plot contributing four rows.\",\"observed_value\":\"Independent unit column: plot_id; 10 plots x 4 visits = 40 rows\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":13,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one static-chamber methane flux reading taken on one peat plot during one survey visit\\nIndependent unit column: plot_id\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":9,\"end_line\":11,\"quoted_text\":\"Because each plot contributes four separate records, plot identifiers repeat\\nthroughout the file; readings sharing a plot identifier come from the same\\npatch of peat.\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":18,\"end_line\":18,\"quoted_text\":\"- `plot_id`: label of the peat plot that was sampled. There are 10 distinct plots and each one appears in four rows.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The input data confirm repeated rows per plot: e.g., PEAT-R1 appears in four consecutive rows (visits V1-V4).\",\"observed_value\":\"4 rows for plot PEAT-R1 (and likewise for the other 9 plots; 40 data rows total)\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"PEAT-R1,restored,V1,-6,7.4\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V2,-5,7.8\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V3,-4,8.2\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V4,-7,8.6\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow pools every row's flux into per-management lists without any reference to plot_id, then feeds those row-level lists to scipy.stats.ttest_ind, a procedure that treats each list element as an independent observation.\",\"observed_value\":\"flux_by_group appends one value per row; ttest_ind called on 20 vs 20 row-level values; dof = 38\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":27,\"end_line\":32,\"quoted_text\":\"def flux_by_group(rows):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Collect the flux readings belonging to each management class.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    grouped = {name: [] for name in GROUP_ORDER}\\n    for row in rows:\\n        grouped[row[GROUP_COLUMN]].append(float(row[FLUX_COLUMN]))\\n    return grouped\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":56,\"end_line\":59,\"quoted_text\":\"    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(\\n        grouped[\\\"restored\\\"], grouped[\\\"drained\\\"], equal_var=True\\n    )\\n    dof = n_res + n_dra - 2\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report explicitly states that each of the 40 rows entered the t-test as one observation, and the selected result reports t(38) with n = 20 per group, confirming that the 10 plots each entered four times.\",\"observed_value\":\"t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44; 20 'measurements' per group vs only 5 plots per group\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":9,\"end_line\":9,\"quoted_text\":\"Flux readings were summarised per management class and compared with a two-sample Student t-test (scipy.stats.ttest_ind, equal variances assumed) in which each of the 40 measurement rows entered the test as one observation. Cohen's d was computed from the pooled standard deviation.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":15,\"end_line\":16,\"quoted_text\":\"| restored | 20 | 12.00 | 2.94 |\\n| drained | 20 | 4.00 | 1.47 |\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":20,\"end_line\":20,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Restored chambers released more methane than drained chambers: mean difference 8.00 mg CH4 m^-2 h^-1 (restored 12.00, drained 4.00), two-sample t-test t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44.\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"A reader could argue that each row is a distinct static-chamber deployment ('on every visit one static chamber was deployed'), suggesting physically separate measurement events; however, the same description states that readings sharing a plot identifier come from the same patch of peat and names plot_id as the independent unit, so this does not establish row-level independence.\",\"observed_value\":\"one fresh chamber deployment per plot per visit\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":7,\"end_line\":8,\"quoted_text\":\"Each plot was visited four times across a single growing season, and on every\\nvisit one static chamber was deployed on that plot to measure methane flux.\"}]},{\"description\":\"Severity mitigation rather than refutation: the plot-level means computable from the data (restored plots ~8, 10, 12, 14, 16; drained plots ~2, 3, 4, 5, 6) are cleanly separated, so a correctly specified plot-level comparison (n = 5 per group) would very likely still find a significant restored > drained difference; the direction of the stated conclusion is robust even though the reported t(38), p-value, and Cohen's d are anti-conservative.\",\"observed_value\":\"example: PEAT-R1 mean 8.0 vs PEAT-D1 mean 2.0; group ranges do not overlap at plot level\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"PEAT-R1,restored,V1,-6,7.4\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V2,-5,7.8\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V3,-4,8.2\\nPEAT-R1,restored,V4,-7,8.6\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":22,\"end_line\":25,\"quoted_text\":\"PEAT-D1,drained,V1,-62,1.7\\nPEAT-D1,drained,V2,-58,1.9\\nPEAT-D1,drained,V3,-55,2.1\\nPEAT-D1,drained,V4,-64,2.3\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I attempted to falsify the verdict three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates or otherwise accounts for plot before testing: flux_by_group() iterates over raw rows and appends one flux value per row, and the string 'plot_id' never appears in workflow/analysis.py, so no aggregation, blocking, or mixed-effects structure exists. (2) I checked whether the group counts could plausibly reflect independent units: the report shows 20 'measurements' per group, but the description and CSV show only 5 plots per group, so 20 can only arise as 5 plots x 4 visits, confirming repeated entry of each unit. (3) I checked whether the rows could be independent despite sharing plot_id: the description explicitly names plot_id as the independent unit and says rows sharing a plot identifier come from the same patch of peat, and the data show strong within-plot clustering (e.g., PEAT-R1: 7.4, 7.8, 8.2, 8.6), so treating rows as independent is not defensible. All three falsification routes failed, so the demonstrated-issue verdict stands; the only surviving caveat is that a correct plot-level analysis would likely preserve the direction of the effect, which does not negate that the reported inferential statistics are computed on pseudoreplicated rows.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-k1-reviewer-fable-48","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:0de3a6061d3bb4056306","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result reports a two-sample Student t-test with t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44 computed on all 40 chamber-visit rows (n = 20 per management class), even though the declared independent unit is plot_id and there are only 10 plots (5 per class), each contributing 4 repeated readings from the same patch of peat. Each independent unit therefore enters the row-independent t-test four times, inflating the effective sample size and degrees of freedom (38 instead of 8 at plot level), so the reported test statistic, p-value, and effect size are not supported at the declared unit of independence. This finding is bounded to the inferential quantities: the direction of the difference (restored > drained) is consistent with plot-level means and is not itself contested.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py never uses the plot_id column: flux_by_group() appends every CSV row's ch4_flux_mg_m2_h value to a per-management list, and stats.ttest_ind() is applied directly to these row-level lists (20 values per group). No aggregation to one value per plot (or any repeated-measures/mixed-model structure) is performed, so each of the 10 independent plots is entered four times into a procedure whose validity assumes each entry is an independent observation.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares the independent unit to be plot_id and states that one row is a single visit-level reading, with each plot contributing four rows.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: plot_id; 10 plots x 4 visits = 40 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":13,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"One row is: one static-chamber methane flux reading taken on one peat plot during one survey visit\nIndependent unit column: plot_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":9,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"Because each plot contributes four separate records, plot identifiers repeat\nthroughout the file; readings sharing a plot identifier come from the same\npatch of peat."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"- `plot_id`: label of the peat plot that was sampled. There are 10 distinct plots and each one appears in four rows."}]},{"description":"The input data confirm repeated rows per plot: e.g., PEAT-R1 appears in four consecutive rows (visits V1-V4).","observed_value":"4 rows for plot PEAT-R1 (and likewise for the other 9 plots; 40 data rows total)","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"PEAT-R1,restored,V1,-6,7.4\nPEAT-R1,restored,V2,-5,7.8\nPEAT-R1,restored,V3,-4,8.2\nPEAT-R1,restored,V4,-7,8.6"}]},{"description":"The workflow pools every row's flux into per-management lists without any reference to plot_id, then feeds those row-level lists to scipy.stats.ttest_ind, a procedure that treats each list element as an independent observation.","observed_value":"flux_by_group appends one value per row; ttest_ind called on 20 vs 20 row-level values; dof = 38","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":27,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"def flux_by_group(rows):\n    \"\"\"Collect the flux readings belonging to each management class.\"\"\"\n    grouped = {name: [] for name in GROUP_ORDER}\n    for row in rows:\n        grouped[row[GROUP_COLUMN]].append(float(row[FLUX_COLUMN]))\n    return grouped"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":56,"end_line":59,"quoted_text":"    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(\n        grouped[\"restored\"], grouped[\"drained\"], equal_var=True\n    )\n    dof = n_res + n_dra - 2"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that each of the 40 rows entered the t-test as one observation, and the selected result reports t(38) with n = 20 per group, confirming that the 10 plots each entered four times.","observed_value":"t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44; 20 'measurements' per group vs only 5 plots per group","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":9,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"Flux readings were summarised per management class and compared with a two-sample Student t-test (scipy.stats.ttest_ind, equal variances assumed) in which each of the 40 measurement rows entered the test as one observation. Cohen's d was computed from the pooled standard deviation."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":15,"end_line":16,"quoted_text":"| restored | 20 | 12.00 | 2.94 |\n| drained | 20 | 4.00 | 1.47 |"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":20,"end_line":20,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Restored chambers released more methane than drained chambers: mean difference 8.00 mg CH4 m^-2 h^-1 (restored 12.00, drained 4.00), two-sample t-test t(38) = 10.89, p < 0.001, Cohen's d = 3.44."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"A reader could argue that each row is a distinct static-chamber deployment ('on every visit one static chamber was deployed'), suggesting physically separate measurement events; however, the same description states that readings sharing a plot identifier come from the same patch of peat and names plot_id as the independent unit, so this does not establish row-level independence.","observed_value":"one fresh chamber deployment per plot per visit","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":7,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"Each plot was visited four times across a single growing season, and on every\nvisit one static chamber was deployed on that plot to measure methane flux."}]},{"description":"Severity mitigation rather than refutation: the plot-level means computable from the data (restored plots ~8, 10, 12, 14, 16; drained plots ~2, 3, 4, 5, 6) are cleanly separated, so a correctly specified plot-level comparison (n = 5 per group) would very likely still find a significant restored > drained difference; the direction of the stated conclusion is robust even though the reported t(38), p-value, and Cohen's d are anti-conservative.","observed_value":"example: PEAT-R1 mean 8.0 vs PEAT-D1 mean 2.0; group ranges do not overlap at plot level","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"PEAT-R1,restored,V1,-6,7.4\nPEAT-R1,restored,V2,-5,7.8\nPEAT-R1,restored,V3,-4,8.2\nPEAT-R1,restored,V4,-7,8.6"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":22,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"PEAT-D1,drained,V1,-62,1.7\nPEAT-D1,drained,V2,-58,1.9\nPEAT-D1,drained,V3,-55,2.1\nPEAT-D1,drained,V4,-64,2.3"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the verdict three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates or otherwise accounts for plot before testing: flux_by_group() iterates over raw rows and appends one flux value per row, and the string 'plot_id' never appears in workflow/analysis.py, so no aggregation, blocking, or mixed-effects structure exists. (2) I checked whether the group counts could plausibly reflect independent units: the report shows 20 'measurements' per group, but the description and CSV show only 5 plots per group, so 20 can only arise as 5 plots x 4 visits, confirming repeated entry of each unit. (3) I checked whether the rows could be independent despite sharing plot_id: the description explicitly names plot_id as the independent unit and says rows sharing a plot identifier come from the same patch of peat, and the data show strong within-plot clustering (e.g., PEAT-R1: 7.4, 7.8, 8.2, 8.6), so treating rows as independent is not defensible. All three falsification routes failed, so the demonstrated-issue verdict stands; the only surviving caveat is that a correct plot-level analysis would likely preserve the direction of the effect, which does not negate that the reported inferential statistics are computed on pseudoreplicated rows.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":32776,"ttft_stream_ms":1416,"time_to_request_ms":12,"type":"result","duration_ms":69715,"uuid":"dfa8c89d-b64e-4b40-953a-58b493193d84"}
